        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change mirrors the existing conventions of this codebase precisely and is a clean, additive extension:\n\n- `RequireCityCapacity` is a faithful sibling of `RequireUniqueHousehold`: same `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto, OwnerRepository)` shape, same `findAll()`-and-filter idiom, same null/guard-then-throw structure, same checked exception + javadoc phrasing (\"The new owner is not persisted yet, so every match found is an existing owner\"). The `Require<X>` naming fits the `RequireUniqueTelephone`/`RequireUniqueHousehold`/`RequireOwnerFields` family exactly.\n- `CityAtCapacityException` and `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler` follow the escalation-package pattern; the handler mirrors `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler`'s `errors`-array/409 shape and correctly reports `[\"city\"]`.\n- The escalation YAML and the `owners.POST.yml` wiring slot the new `capacity` step in with `govern: [ transaction ]` and correct `next:` chaining, consistent with every neighboring step.\n\nLogic is in the right unit (a dedicated one-method function class), nothing is duplicated beyond the intentional convention-matching, and no class is becoming a catch-all. A maintainer of this codebase would find it natural. The `findAll()` scan is the established pattern here (`RequireUniqueHousehold`), so it's consistent rather than a structural deviation \u2014 and efficiency/correctness is out of scope for this review anyway."
